---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Breadcrumb from "@/components/Breadcrumb.astro";
import Main from "@/components/Main.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import config from "@/config";

const pageTitle = "Affiliate Disclosure & Editorial Independence";
const pageDescription =
  "How Red Sun Japan handles affiliate links, sponsorship, and commercial relationships. FTC 16 CFR Part 255 compliance, editorial standards, and how to identify commercial content.";

const contactEmail = "contact@redsunjapan.com";
const privacyEmail = "privacy@redsunjapan.com";
const commercialMailto = `mailto:${contactEmail}?subject=${encodeURIComponent("Commercial / Partnership Inquiry")}`;
const dmcaMailto = `mailto:${privacyEmail}?subject=${encodeURIComponent("DMCA / Commercial Disclosure Concern")}`;
---

<Layout
  title={`${pageTitle} | ${config.site.title}`}
  description={pageDescription}
>
  <Header />

  <Breadcrumb />

  <Main pageTitle={pageTitle} class="app-prose">
    <p class="lead italic text-[#928f88]">
      Last Updated: October 15, 2026. This page discloses how Red Sun
      Japan handles commercial relationships, affiliate links, and any
      compensation that influences the content we publish.
    </p>

    <div
      class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border border-[#27272a] bg-[#161619] p-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-[#928f88]"
    >
      <strong class="text-[#e2ded7]">TL;DR:</strong>
      <strong class="text-[#e2ded7]">We do not run sponsored
        episodes.</strong>
      We do not accept payment to cover a story, suppress a story, or
      shape editorial opinion. If we ever include an affiliate link to a
      book, tool, or piece of art, the channel may earn a small
      commission at
      <strong class="text-[#e2ded7]">no additional cost to you</strong>.
      Every such link is marked.
    </div>

    <h2>1. Regulatory framework</h2>
    <p>
      This page is published in compliance with the
      <strong>U.S. Federal Trade Commission (FTC) Endorsement Guides,
        16 CFR Part 255</strong>, which require clear disclosure of
      "material connections" between an endorser and a seller —
      including affiliate relationships, free product, paid sponsorships,
      and family or employment ties.
    </p>
    <p>
      Equivalent laws that inform this policy include the
      <strong>UK Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) guidance on
        influencer transparency</strong>, the
      <strong>Brazilian CONAR Code of Ethics on Advertising</strong>,
      and <strong>Japan's Act against Unjustifiable Premiums and
        Misleading Representations (景品表示法)</strong>. Where
      jurisdictions diverge, we apply the strictest standard.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Editorial independence — the non-negotiables</h2>
    <p>
      These rules govern every commercial decision the channel makes.
      They are not negotiable and have not changed since the channel
      launched:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>No paid editorial coverage.</strong> We do not accept
        payment, free product, hospitality, or any other consideration
        in exchange for covering a story, an individual, a product, or a
        place.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No sponsored segments inside videos.</strong> Episodes
        do not contain ad reads, product placements, or branded
        integrations. The exception is platform-level mid-roll
        advertising (YouTube's own program), over which we have no
        editorial control.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No advance review by sponsors.</strong> Commercial
        partners never see scripts, narration drafts, or final cuts
        before publication.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Affiliate links do not bias coverage.</strong> If we
        link to a book on a topic, the link does not increase or
        decrease the chance that the book appears in our research. We
        link to things we would have cited anyway.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Corrections are public.</strong> If a commercial
        relationship is later found to have influenced a piece of
        content in a way we did not intend, we will publish a
        correction with the same prominence as the original piece.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>3. What we mean by "affiliate link"</h2>
    <p>
      An affiliate link is a URL that contains a tracking parameter
      identifying the referrer (in our case, Red Sun Japan). If a
      visitor clicks the link and later makes a purchase, the affiliate
      program may pay us a small percentage of the sale. The price you
      pay is the same with or without the link — the commission comes
      out of the merchant's marketing budget, not your pocket.
    </p>
    <p>
      Affiliate revenue is one of the smaller funding lines for the
      channel; it does not cover operating costs and has never been the
      reason an article was written. We disclose it because the law
      requires it, and because the relationship should be visible to
      you.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Categories we may link to (with editorial criteria)</h2>
    <p>
      When we link to a product, it falls into one of these categories.
      Each has a written editorial standard that must be met before the
      link goes live:
    </p>

    <h3>4.1 Books and primary sources</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Classical Japanese literature in translation (Tuttle,
        Princeton, Columbia). Criterion: scholarly translation with
        apparatus, not a paraphrased retelling.
      </li>
      <li>
        Edo-period reference works (kokushi, kaidan collections,
        historical dictionaries). Criterion: directly cited in the
        episode's research.
      </li>
      <li>
        Western-language scholarship that informed the script
        (historians, folklorists, anthropologists). Criterion: the work
        actually appears in the bibliography of the blog post, not a
        generic "related reading" list.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4.2 Tools and craft supplies</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Calligraphy supplies, sumi ink, washi paper. Criterion:
        professional-grade; we will not link to disposable tourist
        souvenirs.
      </li>
      <li>
        Historical reference books and replicas (armor, weapons,
        clothing) sold by museums or specialty vendors. Criterion: the
        vendor provides provenance and is known to the museum-studies
        community.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4.3 Art and merchandise from this site</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        When the channel operates a Printify storefront for fine art
        prints, puzzles, and apparel, every product page is itself a
        commercial disclosure. The art is original to the channel or
        derived from public-domain ukiyo-e and emakimono; the
        commercial relationship is the storefront itself.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Categories we will never link to</h2>
    <p>
      These are lines we will not cross regardless of commission rate,
      audience request, or commercial pressure:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Gambling products, prediction markets, or speculative financial
        products. Japanese history has too many lessons about
        speculative ruin.
      </li>
      <li>
        Supplements, wellness products, or anything ingested. The
        channel does not give health advice.
      </li>
      <li>
        VPN services, password managers, or "online safety" tools that
        profit from fear-based marketing.
      </li>
      <li>
        Any product whose marketing uses imagery of suffering, religious
        disrespect, or trivialization of historical atrocity.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dropshipping or "Amazon FBA aggregator" products with no
        verifiable brand identity.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. How to identify a commercial link on this site</h2>
    <p>
      On the blog, every article that contains an affiliate link carries
      a visible disclosure at the top of the post body, formatted as:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <em
        >"This article contains affiliate links. Red Sun Japan may earn a
        commission on qualifying purchases, at no additional cost to
        you."</em
      >
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      On social platforms, sponsored or affiliate content is tagged
      with the platform-native disclosure tools (e.g. YouTube's
      "Includes paid promotion" toggle, Instagram's "Paid partnership"
      label, X's ad marker). We do not rely on hashtags like
      <code class="text-xs bg-[#222227] px-1 py-0.5 rounded">#ad</code>
      alone, since the platforms' tools are more reliable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Affiliate links themselves are not visually distinguished by
      color on the blog. We rely on the disclosure block at the top of
      the post rather than per-link markers, on the principle that
      "disclosure once, prominently" is more honest than a forest of
      small icons.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Editorial review process for affiliate inclusion</h2>
    <p>
      Before any affiliate link is published in a blog post, the
      editorial team runs the following check:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        The linked product appears in the research bibliography of the
        post, or is a primary source directly cited in the narration.
      </li>
      <li>
        The linked product has been independently reviewed by a
        recognized authority in its category (a museum, a scholarly
        publisher, a professional guild).
      </li>
      <li>
        The vendor discloses provenance, materials, and country of
        origin clearly on the product page.
      </li>
      <li>
        The commission rate is not the reason the product is being
        recommended; the editorial reason stands without the link.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      If any of these checks fail, the link is removed and the post is
      re-published with the non-affiliate URL (or no link at all).
    </p>

    <h2>8. Conflict-of-interest disclosures</h2>
    <p>
      The editorial team is required to disclose, in the blog post or
      video description, any of the following relationships with a
      person, organization, or work that appears in a piece:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Family, romantic, or close personal relationship.</li>
      <li>Current or recent (within 24 months) employment.</li>
      <li>Direct financial holding exceeding 1% of a public company.</li>
      <li>
        Receipt of any gift, hospitality, or free product valued at
        more than USD 50 in the 12 months prior to publication.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When in doubt, we disclose. When disclosure would be
      disproportionate to the size of the relationship, we still
      disclose.
    </p>

    <h2>9. AI-generated content and materials</h2>
    <p>
      Some of the channel's visual assets are AI-assisted. We mark
      those assets individually in the blog post where they appear, and
      we do not link to AI-generation services as a revenue source
      (i.e. we are not an "AI tools affiliate"). If that policy
      changes, this page will be updated before any new links are
      published.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Questions, complaints, and DMCA</h2>
    <p>
      If you believe a post on this site contains an undisclosed
      commercial relationship, please write to
      <a href={dmcaMailto}>{privacyEmail}</a>
      with the URL of the post and a brief explanation. We will review
      and respond within 7 business days; if a correction is warranted,
      it will be published with the same prominence as the original
      post.
    </p>
    <p>
      For general commercial and partnership inquiries (not corrections
      or complaints), write to
      <a href={commercialMailto}>{contactEmail}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Changes to this disclosure</h2>
    <p>
      We will update this page if our commercial relationships change
      in any material way (new affiliate programs, new categories of
      sponsored content, new regulatory requirements). Material changes
      will be reflected in the "Last Updated" date at the top of this
      page and in a short note in the next blog post that includes
      commercial content.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <p class="text-sm text-[#928f88]">
      For the full data-handling and privacy framework, see the
      <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
      For cookie and consent management, see the
      <a href="/cookies/">Cookie Policy</a>.
    </p>
  </Main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
